1. THE BIGGEST GOLD

The most valuable discovery is not any individual protocol.

It is this:

Naya Power must move the important parts of AI behavior OUTSIDE the model and INTO an executable, versioned, verifiable runtime.

That means:

Model = intelligence

GitHub = persistent external brain / source-controlled state

Runtime = enforcement

Human = vision, values, protected elements, and consequential authorization

This distinction appears repeatedly throughout the reviews.

That is the architectural leap.

We are no longer trying to make an AI remember to behave well.

We're building a system where:

excellent behavior is structurally easier, and bad behavior is structurally harder.

That directly validates our deepest principle:

The goal isn't to make AI obedient to Naya Power. The goal is to make excellent behavior harder to fake than mediocre behavior.

The reviews specifically identified this as one of the strongest ideas — while correctly warning that it only becomes real when validators and gates enforce it.

That warning is critical.

2. THE MOST IMPORTANT CORRECTION

All the AIs effectively gave us the same warning:

We have enough philosophy.
We need enforcement.

The recurring failure pattern was:

Good rule → good schema → but nothing mechanically prevents violation.

For example:

evidence_tier: E3

doesn't mean anything if an AI can simply write E3.

The validator has to ask:

Where is the test?

What command ran?

What was actually observed?

Does that evidence establish the success criterion?

Only then can the runtime promote the claim.

The review put this exceptionally clearly: a schema without enforcement is decorative.

Therefore our priority changes.

Not:

Add 50 more protocols.

Instead:

Make the most important 10 protocols mechanically real.

